# `terminate` must stop an *internal* job's underlying execution, not just flip its
# status flag -- confirmed here by backgrounding a two-statement block (`{sleep ...;
# echo SHOULD_NOT_APPEAR} &`), terminating it while the first statement (a long `sleep`)
# is still running, and then confirming the second statement (`echo
# SHOULD_NOT_APPEAR`) never actually ran.
#
# This is deliberately *not* the "status flag" scenario `tests/vt_terminate_and_signal_
# external_job.crush`/Task 9 already cover (those are about an *external* `cmd` job's
# `jobs` row correctly reading `Terminated`). The sibling, narrower bug this test
# exists to catch is real and was confirmed directly: `GlobalState::terminate` already
# marks the targeted job (and every job nested inside it -- see `descendant_job_ids`)
# `Terminated` synchronously, and `sleep` itself (being cooperative -- see
# `tests/terminate.crush`) does stop its own underlying wait almost immediately. But
# `Closure::eval_inner`'s sequential-statement loop used to decide whether to advance
# to the *next* statement purely by checking whether `ThreadStore::join_job` returned
# an error for the one that just ran -- and that join is not race-free: `ThreadStore::
# reap` is called by *any* new job being created anywhere in the system (here, the
# nested session's own next top-level statement, running concurrently with this
# backgrounded block's own worker thread), and it can win the race to consume the
# terminated statement's own thread and its real error first, leaving the closure's
# own, later `join_job` call with nothing left to join -- so it saw no error and wrongly
# ran the next statement anyway. Reproduced directly, 100% of the time across dozens of
# runs, before `Closure::eval_inner` started also checking `JobHandle::is_terminated`
# (a plain, synchronously-set status flag, independent of that race) directly. See that
# method's own doc comment in `src/lang/state/handles.rs` for the full race.
#
# Job id `0` for the backgrounded block: confirmed directly against the debug binary
# (dumping `$t:contents` after the first `jobs` call) -- in a freshly created vt
# session, the first job ever typed gets id 0, same precedent as the sibling vt
# job-control tests (e.g. `vt_bg_resumes_without_foregrounding.crush`).
#
# A settle delay follows each `wait_for "# "` below, same precedent as the sibling vt
# tests (e.g. `vt_terminate_and_signal_external_job.crush`): the prompt text is part of
# the same rendered line a freshly-typed command appears on, so a bare `wait_for "# "`
# can be satisfied by a prompt already on screen before the write actually lands.
$crush_bin := $(crush:env["CRUSH_BIN"])
$t := $(vt:create $crush_bin)
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=300)

$t:write "{sleep $(duration:of seconds=100); echo SHOULD_NOT_APPEAR} &\n"
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=300)

$t:write "terminate 0\n"
$t:wait_for "# "
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=300)

$t:write "jobs\n"
$t:wait_for "Terminated"
sleep $(duration:of milliseconds=1000)

$t:write "echo MARKER\n"
$t:wait_for "MARKER"
assert (!$(like $($t:contents) *SHOULD_NOT_APPEAR*)) "terminate must actually stop the backgrounded block, not just flip its status -- SHOULD_NOT_APPEAR must never run"

$t:close
echo "ok"
